{"name":"Orange Peel","hex":"#ffa000","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orange peel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The skin of an orange, including the pith.","An uneven surface resembling orange peel, especially cellulite or an imperfect finish on a spray-painted object such as a car.\n A surface defect in which sheet metal has a rough texture, similar to an orange peel, due to the metal grains being relatively large."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange_peel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Goode homolosine projection","blocks":[{"text":"The Goode homolosine projection is a pseudocylindrical, equal-area, composite map projection used for world maps. Normally it is presented with multiple interruptions, most commonly of the major oceans. Its equal-area property makes it useful for presenting spatial distribution of phenomena."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Goode_homolosine_projection_SW.jpg/330px-Goode_homolosine_projection_SW.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goode_homolosine_projection"},{"title":"Orange peel (effect)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange peel is a certain kind of finish that may develop on painted and cast surfaces. The texture resembles the surface of the skin of an orange, hence the name \"orange peel\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Orange_Peel_Effect.jpg/330px-Orange_Peel_Effect.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_peel_(effect)"},{"title":"Orange Peel (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange Peel was a Thoroughbred stallion that had a significant influence on the breeding of sport horses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/DEAUVILLE%2C_4_Ao%C3%BBt._%E2%80%94L%27arriv%C3%A9e_du_PRIX_MAURICE_DE_GHEEST_%3B_GRILLEMONT_bat_ais%C3%A9ment_ORANGE_PEEL_et_SOLANGE.jpg/330px-DEAUVILLE%2C_4_Ao%C3%BBt._%E2%80%94L%27arriv%C3%A9e_du_PRIX_MAURICE_DE_GHEEST_%3B_GRILLEMONT_bat_ais%C3%A9ment_ORANGE_PEEL_et_SOLANGE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_Peel_(horse)"},{"title":"Orange (fruit)","blocks":[{"text":"The orange, also called sweet orange to distinguish it from the bitter orange, is the fruit of a tree in the family Rutaceae. Botanically, this is the hybrid Citrus × sinensis, between the pomelo and the mandarin orange. The chloroplast genome, and therefore the maternal line, is that of pomelo. Hybrids of the sweet orange form later types of mandarin and the grapefruit. The sweet orange has had its full genome sequenced."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg/330px-Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(fruit)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Orange_Blossom.JPG/330px-Orange_Blossom.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Orange_Blossom.JPG/960px-Orange_Blossom.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Orange_Blossom.JPG/330px-Orange_Blossom.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrange_Blossom.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Orange_tree_fruiting.jpg/250px-Orange_tree_fruiting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Orange_tree_fruiting.jpg/500px-Orange_tree_fruiting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig